# 14. FIRESTORE DATABASE DESIGN

Use a structured Firestore schema.

Suggested collections:

users/{uid}

users/{uid}/providers/{providerId}

users/{uid}/models/{modelId}

users/{uid}/combos/{comboId}

users/{uid}/routingRules/{ruleId}

users/{uid}/apiKeys/{keyId}

users/{uid}/requestLogs/{requestId}

users/{uid}/usageDaily/{date}

users/{uid}/healthChecks/{checkId}

users/{uid}/settings/config

Store sensitive provider configuration encrypted.

Store gateway API key hashes, never raw keys.

Use server-side validation and ownership checks.

Create appropriate indexes.

Implement pagination for request logs.

Use Firestore transactions or atomic operations for counters where appropriate.

Avoid unnecessary high-frequency writes.

Implement a retention policy for old logs.

### Firestore Security

- Only the authorized owner may access the dashboard.
- Never allow public database access.
- Deny unauthorized client writes to protected collections.
- Validate document ownership.
- Do not trust client-provided role or UID values.
- Use Firebase Admin SDK on the server with IAM-controlled access.
- Remember that Admin SDK operations bypass Firestore Security Rules.
- Configure server-side authorization independently.
- Test security rules before production deployment.

